      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To provide information specific to a catecholaminergic storm during a violent stress in children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Two clinical observations in children who suffered violent trauma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS"
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "FIRST CASE",
            "content": "a boy aged 12 years victim of a drowning in Stage 3 admitted to the pediatric intensive care 02.08.2015. He was intubated, ventilated, sedated with benzodiazepines and morphine. Cardiac exploration revealed a global hypokinesis with septal dyskinesia, a 45% FES offset a slight excess of ST, a troponin I to 31.46pg/mL NT-proBNB to 1291pg/mL. Dobutamine was set initially at 7 then 10 gamma/kg/min. The outcome was favorable on the heart level with improved SEF in 1 week."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SECOND CASE",
            "content": "13-year-old girl, victim of complete hanging, admitted on 06/08/2015 for a post-anoxic encephalopathy. Intubated, ventilated and sedated with morphine and benzodiazepines. Cardiac exploration showed septal hypokinesia with a 35% FES, a lengthening of the QT interval on the ECG, a troponin I in ultra 1.27μg/L, NT-proBNB to 215pg/mL. The child was put up 15 gamma dobutamine/kg/min. The evolution was marked by improved heart function without disturbance wall motion after 7 days."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Our 2 clinical cases following accidental trauma violating represent observations of a picture simulating a syndrome of Tako-Tsubo without anginal pain objectified as our 2 patients were sedated and ultrasound abnormalities returned to normal in less than a week."
          }
        ]
      },
